# KGB-CIA: every environment variable the pipeline can read, and the step that needs it.
#
# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in what you have, then run with
#     uv run --env-file .env <command>
# or export the variables from your shell or a keychain lookup. Scripts only ever read
# environment variables, so any of those work. On the AWS server the same names are set from
# SSM Parameter Store. Never commit a key.
#
# Nothing below is needed to build the graph (kgb-transform), clean it (kgb-clean), cut a
# subgraph (kgb-filter), or serve it (kgb-mcp): those stages, NodeNorm and PubMed need no key.

# --- Augmentation (kgb-augment, pubmed-evidence skill): RENCI Azure OpenAI models -------------
# The key RENCI issued for the analytics endpoint. These are Pi's standard Azure names, so the
# Pi provider docs apply; the model list is in .pi/models.json. If you also use Azure OpenAI
# for something else, set these per project rather than globally.
AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Defaults to the RENCI endpoint; set only to point at another deployment.
AZURE_OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://renci-analytics.openai.azure.com/openai/v1

# --- Cleaning, evidence check (evidence-check skill): TypeSafe AI, the Jev model -------------
# Without it the skill writes the requests it would send and a cost estimate, and sends nothing.
TYPESAFE_API_KEY=

# --- Your agent harness, if it is Claude (Claude Code, or Pi with an Anthropic provider) ------
# Not read by any pipeline script; the harness reads it. Pi users on the RENCI models don't need it.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- PubMed (augmentation and the evidence check), optional ----------------------------------
# Without a key E-utilities allows 3 requests a second; with one, 10. Register at NCBI.
NCBI_API_KEY=
# NCBI asks for a contact address on bulk requests.
NCBI_EMAIL=
# Shared search and abstract cache (shared/pubmed, #52). Point both agents at one file so the
# second run reuses what the first fetched; the RENCI drive is the agreed place.
KGB_PUBMED_CACHE=data/cache/pubmed.sqlite
